WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.270
This was the center of Marawi.

00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:07.760
Today, residents call it "ground zero".

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:12.780
We get the military's permission to enter
selected parts and are warned about unexploded

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:15.260
devices.

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:21.240
Last year a few hundred 'IS'-inspired fighters
holed up in mosques and houses.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:30.220
The army, with help from the US, responded
with relentless air raids.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:36.460
In this type of warfare you have to flush
out the enemy from their defensive position

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:43.329
and you can only do that Number 1 using bombs
or heavy artillery bombardment.

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:51.329
If you look at the experience of Mosul, Raqqa
and Aleppo, all of these experienced bombings.

00:00:51.329 --> 00:01:00.070
But in the case of Marawi City, when we did
the bombings, we did it pinpointed to specific

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:03.590
targets only.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:09.389
But people here paid a very high price for
the 5-month-long battle.

00:01:09.389 --> 00:01:11.189
Hundreds were killed.

00:01:11.189 --> 00:01:14.100
They're buried in these mass graves.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:20.759
Most of the victims were never identified,
denying their families closure.

00:01:20.759 --> 00:01:24.950
Over 65,000 people remain displaced.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:28.109
Many are still searching for their loved ones.

00:01:28.109 --> 00:01:31.090
Fatima has lost four of her eight children.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:38.549
She believes they were taken hostage and used
as human shields.

00:01:38.549 --> 00:01:41.259
I feel a very deep pain inside of me.

00:01:41.259 --> 00:01:43.039
I miss my children.

00:01:43.039 --> 00:01:44.939
They were gone in a blink of an eye.

00:01:44.939 --> 00:01:49.119
I can't be with them any more.

00:01:49.119 --> 00:01:51.749
It hurts, because I can still feel them and
hear them.

00:01:51.749 --> 00:02:01.149
In the suburbs, life is slowly returning to
normal, but the biggest Muslim city in the

00:02:01.149 --> 00:02:07.099
Philippines has been changed forever.

00:02:07.099 --> 00:02:10.920
Martial law remains in force, with soldiers
everywhere.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:16.310
The impoverished region has witnessed decades
of conflict between Muslims and Christians

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:21.219
who make up the vast majority of the Filipino
population.

00:02:21.219 --> 00:02:27.720
Many young Muslims feel disenfranchised.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:34.030
Last year Abdul spied on the military, passing
on details of troop movements to 'IS' fighters

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:35.269
holed up in Marawi.

00:02:35.269 --> 00:02:45.420
He tells us that he's still ready to fight
for an Islamic state.

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.719
We have an advantage because we just blend
in.

00:02:48.719 --> 00:02:59.150
The military cannot easily identify us as
fighters who know how to use a gun.

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:03.609
I would not fight in Marawi again, or in any
other Muslim area here.

00:03:03.609 --> 00:03:06.249
Then the same thing will happen again.

00:03:06.249 --> 00:03:09.650
Our own Muslim people would get hurt.

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:17.519
But if the battle happens elsewhere, I will
join.

00:03:17.519 --> 00:03:23.010
Like many others, Abdul blames President Duterte
for bombing Marawi.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:27.469
The government has promised reconstruction
and greater Muslim autonomy.

00:03:27.469 --> 00:04:37.580
But until those promises are fulfilled, angry
young men like Abdul continue to pose

00:04:37.580 --> 00:06:23.770
a threat.

